/* ==========================================================================
   MATCH RESULT

   The screen a match ends on. It was a TextMeshPro label on a canvas of its
   own while everything else moved to UI Toolkit, so it kept a font, a scale
   and a set of colours that stopped matching the rest of the game.

   It has no panel, and that is the whole shape of it. A panel is a card put in
   front of the game because the game is still there to go back to - a pause
   menu, the settings, a prompt. This is the game being over: the frame goes
   dark and the sentence is written on the dark.

   The screen layer already centres what is on it and already carries the veil,
   so there is nothing here about either.
   ========================================================================== */

.result__outcome {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);

    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);

    margin-bottom: var(--space-m);

    white-space: normal;

    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}


/* Rust is the one place the quiet breaks in this palette, and everybody being
   caught is the one thing in a match worth breaking it for. A win is left in
   the ordinary colour: it is the outcome the game is about, and shouting it
   would make the other one quieter by comparison. */
.result--defeat .result__outcome {
    color: var(--danger);
}


/* Bigger than a hint anywhere else in the game. A hint is normally a note
   beside something a player is doing and can be small because they are looking
   at the thing, not at it; here there is nothing else on the screen, and next
   to a sentence at fifty-six it read as fine print. */
.result__note {
    font-size: var(--font-size-m);

    margin-bottom: 0;

    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}
